00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.560
that were completely beyond his control.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.060
All of that aside, the
damaging ramifications

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.250
of the do what you love mentality

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.220
have similarly been explored
by a host of recent writers.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.053
In her book,

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:57.980
"Can't Even: How Millennials
Became the Burnout Generation"

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:59.777
writer Anne Helen Petersen says,

00:05:59.777 --> 00:06:01.527
"The rhetoric of do what you love

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:03.347
"and you'll never work
another day in your life

00:06:03.347 --> 00:06:04.807
"is a burnout trap.

00:06:04.807 --> 00:06:07.667
"By cloaking the labor in
the language of passion,

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.977
"we're prevented from
thinking of what we do

00:06:09.977 --> 00:06:14.710
"as what it is: A job, not
the entirety of our lives."

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.590
Of course, Joe stands in
stark contrast to this.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.830
His whole mission is to turn
his passion into his career.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:22.870
And later in the film we have some hints

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.270
that Joe has been avoiding
other aspects of his life

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.820
in pursuit of that career.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.620
- I'm telling you, Joe, you
really should call Lisa again.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.050
- [Joe] I don't really have
time for relationship right now.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.280
- In this way he's hardly an outlier.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.447
As Miya Tokumitsu, author of
"Do What You Love" rights,

00:06:37.447 --> 00:06:39.887
"Work has held up as something
that is more regulatory

00:06:39.887 --> 00:06:42.067
"about your character
than the interest you have

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:43.787
